<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I, Duke of Mecklenburg-Stargard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1326–<a href="/years/1392">1392</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-lord-of-mecklenburg">Henry II of Mecklenburg</a>. In 1348 he and his brother Albert II were raised by the emperor Charles IV from lords to dukes of Mecklenburg, and in their division of 1352 John received the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lordship-of-stargard">Lordship of Stargard</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg">Neubrandenburg</a>. He founded the line of Mecklenburg-Stargard, which ruled until it died out in 1471 and its lands returned to the main line.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was probably born in 1326, the youngest child of the second marriage of Henry II the Lion of Mecklenburg to Anna of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, a daughter of Duke Albert II of Saxe-Wittenberg. His father died in 1329, and John remained under guardianship until 1344, when he came of age and began to bear a seal as a partner in the government of Mecklenburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> raised John and his brother Albert II to the rank of duke at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 8 July 1348. The brothers first backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/false-waldemar">False Waldemar</a> against Margrave Louis V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, but made peace with Louis in 1350 after the emperor dropped Waldemar. In the division of Mecklenburg on 25 November 1352 John received the lordships of Stargard, Sternberg, Eldenbürg (Lübz) and Ture. He supported his nephew Albert III of Mecklenburg in his efforts to be recognised as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p>John married three times. His first wife, Rixa, probably died soon after the wedding. His second, Anna, daughter of Count Adolf VII of Pinneberg and Schauenburg, probably died in 1358 and bore a daughter, Anna, who married Wartislaw VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a> in 1363. With his third wife Agnes, daughter of Ulrich II of Lindow-Ruppin and widow of Nicholas IV of Werle, he had five children, among them his successors John II and Ulrich I and Rudolf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Skara and later of Schwerin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The historian Nikolaus Marschalk, who died in 1525, placed John's death in the 1370s and his burial in the cathedral at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, but documents show him active into the 1390s. The last known document bearing his signature is dated 9 August 1392, and by 9 February 1393 his sons John II and Ulrich I were ruling in his place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John I of Mecklenburg-Stargard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1352 the brothers Albert II and <b>John I</b>, raised to dukes of Mecklenburg in 1348, divided their lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-stargard">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Stargard</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schwerin, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Burg Stargard, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.63,11.42&amp;year=1368" aria-label="Schwerin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4804 321 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John I, Duke of Mecklenburg-Stargard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 591h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 591h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.63,11.42&amp;year=1368">What was here in 1368? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
